moglobinopathies Topics-in-Focus program\nCongress\nHemaSphere\nAbout EHA\nSearch\nJoin EHA\nLogin\nEHA Research Mobility Grant\nHome\nResearch & Innovation\nGrants\nEHA Research Mobility Grant\nApply for an EHA Research Mobility Grant\nThe call has been extended. You can submit your application through the EHA Portal until 16:00 (CET) on November 30, 2026.\nApplications will be reviewed as they are received, and successful candidates will be notified within two weeks of submission.\nLeft Sidebar\n3 min read · May 6, 2025\nMay 6, 2025\nShare\nGrants\nEHA Research Grants\nEHA Bilateral Collaborative Grant\nEHA Innovation Grant\nEHA Kick-off Grant\nEHA Research Mobility Grant\nHow to apply for an EHA Research Mobility Grant\nEHA Research Mobility Grant review and selection process\nEHA Grants Hall of Fame\nEHA Travel Grants\nNon-EHA funding opportunities\nScope of our grants\nEHA Research Mobility Grants are designed to support the professional development of young scientists by enabling mobility and facilitating a visit to a research group in another institute. Both institutes should have complementary expertise, as this visit is also intended to promote collaboration between the institutes.\nSuccessful applicants can receive up to €7,500 within Europe and €10,000 outside of Europe to support mobility that can last up to three months.\nEligibility\nYou’re eligible if:\nYou’re an EHA member\nThe home and host institutes are academic research centers or proven equivalents\nThe home and host institutes are in different countries and at least one of them is in Europe\nYou have the support of an academic department and institute\nYour application relates to basic and translational lab-based research in hematology\nNote: Purely clinical research projects, projects in health economics, and other non-lab-based research are ineligible.\nIn addition, at least one of the following must apply:\nYou are a PhD student\nYou received your PhD within one year of the current application deadline\nYou are an MD in training (with residency and within one year of hematology accreditation), with a keen interest in pursuing a career in research\nIf you received your qualifications outside the stated timeframe, please check our policy on life events and eligibility.\nWhat an EHA Research Mobility Grant covers\nAn EHA Research Mobility Grant covers:\nA visit of up to three months\nTravel and accommodation expenses up to €7,500 for travel within Europe and up to €10,000 outside Europe\nA small contribution towards lab consumables—which must be approved by the EHA Grants Manager before you submit your application\nIf the project requires a visit longer than three months, you can ask for an extension for up to six months. In this case, you must provide an explanation on your application form. Please contact the EHA Grants Manager ([email protected]) for more details.\nAn EHA Research Mobility Grant does not cover:\nMobility that lasts longer than six months\nOverhead costs\nYour salary\nTravel support for other people\nFunding for existing projects\nPurchase of equipment or other items supporting your stay at the host institute (such as furniture, car, computer, etc.)\nIf this grant does not fully cover your expenses, you must share details of how you’ll cover them on your application form.\nNote: All expenditures must be documented with original receipts, vouchers, or invoices.\nHow to apply for an EHA Research Mobility Grant\nEHA Research Mobility Grant review and selection process\nShare\nGrants\nEHA Research Grants\nEHA Bilateral Collaborative Grant\nEHA Innovation Grant\nEHA Kick-off Grant\nEHA Research Mobility Grant\nHow to apply for an EHA Research Mobility Grant\nEHA Research Mobility Grant review and selection process\nEHA Grants Hall of Fame\nEHA Travel Grants\nNon-EHA funding opportunities\nScope of our grants\nJoin EHA\nFull membership\nJunior membership\n60-day trial\nGet involved\nCareer development\nScientific meetings\nAbout us\nMission a